e challenger, and every expert rater and both major prediction markets agree the race is safely Democratic. With no general-election polling available, the fundamentals and unanimous external ratings carry the forecast, and nothing in the evidence justifies moving away from them.", "watch": "The lieutenant governor scandal and low primary turnout hint at voter frustration; if that sentiment consolidates behind Cordery or Green's approval slips further, the margin could narrow even if the outcome likely would not flip.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Josh Green (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Josh Green enters the general election as a heavy favorite for a second term. Hawaii leans Democratic by about 13 points, Green won the office by roughly 16 points in 2022, and he cleared his primary easily in August. His Republican opponent, contractor Gary Cordery, has shown little fundraising activity or outside support and remains largely undefined to voters.\n\nEvery major race rater, from the Cook Political Report to Sabato's Crystal Ball, places Hawaii in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets give Green roughly a 97 to 98 percent chance of winning. The main open questions are the size of the margin rather than the winner: low primary turnout and a lieutenant governor scandal suggest some voter frustration, and Green's handling of housing and cost-of-living issues could shape how close the final tally gets.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9885, "margin": 16.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9882, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9868, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9794, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.985, "margin": 16.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9851, "margin": 16.5}]}